				<r3d:description language="eng">‘IndExs’ is a biological database and research data repository containing information on exsiccatae and exsiccata-like herbarium specimen series from around the world, including titles, abbreviations, descriptions of contents, bibliographic details and publication information. As a authority control database, it assigns a unique and permanent exsiccata ID to each series. Exsiccatae are reference collections in herbaria and are defined as ‘published, uniform, numbered sets of preserved specimens bearing printed labels’; see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exsiccata and https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=IndExs_%E2%80%93_Index_of_Exsiccatae. They often have separately published booklets with lists of species and specimens distributed, sometimes with taxonomic novelties included. The IndExs database provides access to unique insights into the history of botany and mycology, taxonomy, geobotany, bryology, phycology, cecidology, lichenology and mycology (see https://istc.cetaf.org/Storytelling_with_IndExs). For most exsiccatae, images of one or more representative labels from 150 herbaria worldwide are available. The database’s SQL engine, with its rich client user interface, was designed and configured as a module of the Diversity Workbench. The REST API of the IndExs web service enables machine-assisted searches across more than 2,600 exsiccata series and their respective publishers – totalling more than 1,300. The service is particularly useful as a source of information and a controlled vocabulary for organising data generation and management in herbarium digitisation projects involving historical specimens.</r3d:description>
